raight back. E5 lost 8.1 points and dropped the lead from +3.9 to -4.2. The engine's choice was D4, and White took that point itself on the next move at no cost. The stones at E5, E6 and E7 became a wall, but a wall facing White's strength on the left."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "B4 lost 7.6 points, taking the lead from -5.3 to -12.9. Black pushed along the fourth line against White's stones at C5 and D4, while the engine's choice was F2. White answered B5 and the left side was closed off."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "A4 was the worst move of the game for GPT-6.1 Sol, losing 10.3 points and moving the lead from -5.3 to -15.6. It extended the same edge crawl, and the 20k's A5 cost 10.3 points in reply, which put the lead back at -5.3. After that exchange Black never got closer than -3.6."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "F7 lost 6.2 points, from -6.0 to -12.2, and the engine preferred F8. White's reply at F8 settled the top, and from there the lead stayed near -12 through the final passes."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every move was preceded by a call to inspectLiberties (current-position-liberties.js) plus a check() wrapper that applies only the one move the player had already named, to report resulting liberties and captures. The remaining code was a region/flood-fill count of the final board, a replay to verify the game record, and note-writing; none of it generated, ranked or looked ahead over candidate moves.", "stop": false}}